          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hablas español can assert “You speak Spanish.” Now compare Quiero que hables español — “I want you to speak Spanish.” Hables does not report what is true. It presents the speaking as wanted and not yet asserted. That meaning contrast is the first small doorway into the subjunctive."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: count the doers",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "meaning",
                "Spanish",
                "doers"
              ],
              "columns": 3,
              "rowCount": 2,
              "utterances": [
                "meaning: I want to speak Spanish. Spanish: Quiero hablar español. doers: one: I want; I speak.",
                "meaning: I want you to speak Spanish. Spanish: Quiero que hables español. doers: two: I want; you speak."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "With one doer, keep the known infinitive hablar. With a different doer for the wanted event, use que and the new form hables. This is a bounded pattern for querer, not a rule for every clause in Spanish."
            }
          ]
        },
